Looking at this problem, I need to assess what's being asked.

The problem is "Existence of Odd Weird Numbers" from the Formal Conjectures project. A weird number is a natural number that is abundant (sum of proper divisors exceeds the number) but not semiperfect (no subset of proper divisors sums exactly to the number).

The question of whether **odd** weird numbers exist is a well-known **open problem** in number theory, originally posed by Benkoski and Erdős. All known weird numbers (70, 836, 4030, 5830, ...) are even. Despite extensive computational searches and theoretical work, no odd weird number has ever been found, and no proof of their existence or non-existence has been established.

The Formal Conjectures project formalizes conjectures that are believed to be true but remain unproven — this is precisely such a conjecture. Since this is an open problem in mathematics, I cannot provide a proof.

